      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During the Aethelgard Phase II trial, AE-99's efficacy in reducing inflammation was quantified as a significant mean decrease of 3.46-fold relative to a placebo control showing a negligible decrease of 0.34-fold (95% CI [1.41%, 6.43%], p<0.001). The trial reported mild nausea in 3.8% of male participants aged 60-70 years and 2.9% of female participants in the same age bracket, indicating that the adverse event profile remained consistent and contained within an acceptable safety margin. Moreover, the targeted demographic, which constituted men and women aged 60-70 years, exhibited a mean inflammation marker reduction of 3.15 and 3.55-fold, respectively, and a subpopulation analysis identified an adjusted relative efficacy enhancement of 98% when compared to participants above 70 years (95% CI [94.2%, 101.8%], p<0.001). The robust nature of these results provides compelling evidence supporting AE-99's potential for application in the specified age group, warranting further investigational studies to affirm its clinical utility. Further regression analyses revealed a confidence interval for efficacy that overwhelmingly supports a progressive trend",
      "source": "target",
